WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.450
How can we add pieces when the pieces have different names?

00:00:04.100 --> 00:00:07.312
Today we are adding and subtracting fractions.

00:00:07.532 --> 00:00:11.724
We will use equal-sized wholes and rename parts when we need to.

00:00:11.944 --> 00:00:15.351
What should happen to the size of the pieces when we combine them?

00:00:16.001 --> 00:00:18.611
First, use matching denominators.

00:00:18.831 --> 00:00:22.272
Two sevenths plus three sevenths makes five sevenths.

00:00:22.922 --> 00:00:25.982
We add the numerators and keep the denominator.

00:00:26.202 --> 00:00:29.367
We are still counting sevenths, not fourteenths.

00:00:30.017 --> 00:00:35.340
For one half plus one quarter, the denominators are related: four is a multiple of two.

00:00:35.560 --> 00:00:37.760
Rename the half as two quarters.

00:00:38.410 --> 00:00:41.152
Now add two quarters and one quarter.

00:00:41.372 --> 00:00:43.474
The result is three quarters.

00:00:44.124 --> 00:00:46.688
Subtraction follows the same idea.

00:00:46.908 --> 00:00:51.316
For three quarters minus one eighth, rename three quarters as six eighths.

00:00:51.966 --> 00:00:53.640
Take away one eighth.

00:00:53.860 --> 00:00:55.567
Five eighths remain.

00:00:55.787 --> 00:00:57.844
The denominator stays eight.

00:00:58.494 --> 00:01:02.421
On a number line, begin at six eighths and move back one eighth.

00:01:02.641 --> 00:01:04.643
You land on five eighths.

00:01:05.293 --> 00:01:07.099
Simplify when you can.

00:01:07.319 --> 00:01:12.125
One sixth plus three sixths is four sixths, which equals two thirds.

00:01:12.775 --> 00:01:15.038
Mixed numbers can make another whole.

00:01:15.258 --> 00:01:19.295
One and four fifths plus two fifths is one and six fifths.

00:01:19.945 --> 00:01:22.407
Regroup five fifths as one whole.

00:01:22.627 --> 00:01:25.256
The answer is two and one fifth.

00:01:25.906 --> 00:01:29.568
For several fractions, rename them with a common denominator.

00:01:29.788 --> 00:01:35.383
One half plus one quarter minus one eighth becomes four eighths plus two eighths minus one eighth.

00:01:36.033 --> 00:01:40.277
Four plus two minus one is five, so the result is five eighths.

00:01:40.927 --> 00:01:46.368
Here is an extension: when neither denominator is a multiple of the other, shared multiples can help.

00:01:46.588 --> 00:01:49.084
Try one quarter plus one sixth.

00:01:53.084 --> 00:01:56.349
Multiples of four include four, eight, twelve.

00:01:56.569 --> 00:02:00.239
Multiples of six include six, twelve, eighteen.

00:02:00.459 --> 00:02:03.906
Twelve is the smallest positive shared multiple.

00:02:04.556 --> 00:02:06.865
One quarter is three twelfths.

00:02:07.085 --> 00:02:09.312
One sixth is two twelfths.

00:02:09.532 --> 00:02:11.479
Add to get five twelfths.

00:02:12.129 --> 00:02:14.844
What is two thirds minus one sixth?

00:02:18.844 --> 00:02:21.145
Two thirds is four sixths.

00:02:21.365 --> 00:02:25.453
Four sixths minus one sixth is three sixths, or one half.

00:02:26.453 --> 00:02:30.027
Use the same-sized whole and same-sized parts.

00:02:30.247 --> 00:02:35.797
Rename fractions when needed, combine or remove parts, and check the size of the result.

00:02:36.017 --> 00:02:40.931
Shared-denominator methods beyond related denominators are extension work.
